sharpenedFix:
Take the proposed line-box predicate, but close the hole it leaves in the guest states. As written, `parseInt(cs.webkitLineClamp, 10)` is NaN wherever b.css:259-268 sets -webkit-line-clamp:none, so max > 0 is false and phone/desk/day/print fall straight back to the broken height comparison - I measured the proposed fix leaving desk at 7/7 titles trimmed to one word at a 24px root. When there is no clamp and overflow is visible, nothing is ever hidden, so there is nothing to trim for and the vertical test must not run at all:

  function over(el) {
    if (el.scrollWidth > el.clientWidth + 1) return true;
    var cs = getComputedStyle(el);
    var lh = parseFloat(cs.lineHeight);
    var max = parseInt(cs.webkitLineClamp, 10);
    if (!(lh > 0)) return el.scrollHeight > el.clientHeight + 1;
    if (max > 0) return el.scrollHeight > (max + 0.5) * lh;
    return false;
  }

Measured against the real DOM at a 24px root this leaves 0/7 trimmed on phone, desk and print and 0/8 on day, restores all seven owner titles at 1440, and still clamps a 100-character title to exactly two lines at 390, 768 and 1440 at both roots. Then lock it: add a root-font-size axis to interaction-check.mjs - re-run the existing word-safe block and the guest-title assertion (interaction-check.mjs:2199) at roots 16 and 24 - and replace the overY <= 1 criterion at interaction-check.mjs:314 with a line count against the computed clamp, since the current criterion is the defect's own definition of correct and will keep passing whatever this bug does next.
